---@field CpuRealtimePeriod? number The length of a CPU real-time period in microseconds. Set to 0 to allocate no time allocated to real-time tasks.
---@field CpuRealtimeRuntime? number The length of a CPU real-time runtime in microseconds. Set to 0 to allocate no time allocated to real-time tasks.
---@field CpusetCpus? string CPUs in which to allow execution (e.g., `0-3`, `0,1`).
---@field CpusetMems? string Memory nodes (MEMs) in which to allow execution (0-3, 0,1). Only effective on NUMA systems.
---@field Devices? docker.HostConfigDevicesList A list of devices to add to the container.
---@field DeviceCgroupRules? docker.HostConfigDeviceCgroupRulesList a list of cgroup rules to apply to the container
---@field DeviceRequests? docker.HostConfigDeviceRequestsList A list of requests for devices to be sent to device drivers.
---@field MemoryReservation? number Memory soft limit in bytes.
---@field MemorySwap? number Total memory limit (memory + swap). Set as `-1` to enable unlimited swap.
---@field MemorySwappiness? number Tune a container's memory swappiness behavior. Accepts an integer between 0 and 100.
---@field NanoCpus? number CPU quota in units of 10<sup>-9</sup> CPUs.
---@field OomKillDisable? boolean Disable OOM Killer for the container.
---@field Init? boolean|distilled.Null Run an init inside the container that forwards signals and reaps processes. This field is omitted if empty, and the default (as configured on the daemon) is used.
---@field PidsLimit? number|distilled.Null Tune a container's PIDs limit. Set `0` or `-1` for unlimited, or `null` to not change.
---@field Ulimits? docker.HostConfigUlimitsList A list of resource limits to set in the container. For example: ``` {"Name": "nofile", "Soft": 1024, "Hard": 2048} ```
---@field CpuCount? number The number of usable CPUs (Windows only). On Windows Server containers, the processor resource controls are mutually exclusive. The order of precedence is `CPUCount` first, then `CPUShares`, and `CPUPercent` last.
---@field CpuPercent? number The usable percentage of the available CPUs (Windows only). On Windows Server containers, the processor resource controls are mutually exclusive. The order of precedence is `CPUCount` first, then `CPUShares`, and `CPUPercent` last.
---@field IOMaximumIOps? number Maximum IOps for the container system drive (Windows only)
---@field IOMaximumBandwidth? number Maximum IO in bytes per second for the container system drive (Windows only).
---@field Binds? docker.HostConfigBindsList A list of volume bindings for this container. Each volume binding is a string in one of these forms: - `host-src:container-dest[:options]` to bind-mount a host path into the container. Both `host-src`, and `container-dest` must be an _absolute_ path. - `volume-name:container-dest[:options]` to bind-mount a volume managed by a volume driver into the container. `container-dest` must be an _absolute_ path. `options` is an optional, comma-delimited list of: - `nocopy` disables automatic copying of data from the container path to the volume. The `nocopy` flag only applies to named volumes. - `[ro|rw]` mounts a volume read-only or read-write, respectively. If omitted or set to `rw`, volumes are mounted read-write. - `[z|Z]` applies SELinux labels to allow or deny multiple containers to read and write to the same volume. - `z`: a _shared_ content label is applied to the content. This label indicates that multiple containers can share the volume content, for both reading and writing. - `Z`: a _private unshared_ label is applied to the content. This label indicates that only the current container can use a private volume. Labeling systems such as SELinux require proper labels to be placed on volume content that is mounted into a container. Without a label, the security system can prevent a container's processes from using the content. By default, the labels set by the host operating system are not modified. - `[[r]shared|[r]slave|[r]private]` specifies mount [propagation behavior](https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/filesystems/sharedsubtree.txt). This only applies to bind-mounted volumes, not internal volumes or named volumes. Mount propagation requires the source mount point (the location where the source directory is mounted in the host operating system) to have the correct propagation properties. For shared volumes, the source mount point must be set to `shared`. For slave volumes, the mount must be set to either `shared` or `slave`.
---@field ContainerIDFile? string Path to a file where the container ID is written
---@field LogConfig? docker.HostConfigLogConfig The logging configuration for this container
---@field NetworkMode? string Network mode to use for this container. Supported standard values are: `bridge`, `host`, `none`, and `container:<name|id>`. Any other value is taken as a custom network's name to which this container should connect to.
---@field PortBindings? docker.PortMap
---@field RestartPolicy? docker.RestartPolicy
---@field AutoRemove? boolean Automatically remove the container when the container's process exits. This has no effect if `RestartPolicy` is set.
---@field VolumeDriver? string Driver that this container uses to mount volumes.
---@field VolumesFrom? docker.HostConfigVolumesFromList A list of volumes to inherit from another container, specified in the form `<container name>[:<ro|rw>]`.
---@field Mounts? docker.HostConfigMountsList Specification for mounts to be added to the container.
---@field ConsoleSize? docker.HostConfigConsoleSizeList|distilled.Null Initial console size, as an `[height, width]` array.
---@field Annotations? docker.HostConfigAnnotationsMap Arbitrary non-identifying metadata attached to container and provided to the runtime when the container is started.
---@field CapAdd? docker.HostConfigCapAddList A list of kernel capabilities to add to the container. Conflicts with option 'Capabilities'.
---@field CapDrop? docker.HostConfigCapDropList A list of kernel capabilities to drop from the container. Conflicts with option 'Capabilities'.
---@field CgroupnsMode? docker.HostConfigCgroupnsMode|string cgroup namespace mode for the container. Possible values are: - `"private"`: the container runs in its own private cgroup namespace - `"host"`: use the host system's cgroup namespace If not specified, the daemon default is used, which can either be `"private"` or `"host"`, depending on daemon version, kernel support and configuration.
---@field Dns? docker.HostConfigDnsList A list of DNS servers for the container to use.
---@field DnsOptions? docker.HostConfigDnsOptionsList A list of DNS options.
---@field DnsSearch? docker.HostConfigDnsSearchList A list of DNS search domains.
---@field ExtraHosts? docker.HostConfigExtraHostsList A list of hostnames/IP mappings to add to the container's `/etc/hosts` file. Specified in the form `["hostname:IP"]`.
---@field GroupAdd? docker.HostConfigGroupAddList A list of additional groups that the container process will run as.
---@field IpcMode? string IPC sharing mode for the container. Possible values are: - `"none"`: own private IPC namespace, with /dev/shm not mounted - `"private"`: own private IPC namespace - `"shareable"`: own private IPC namespace, with a possibility to share it with other containers - `"container:<name|id>"`: join another (shareable) container's IPC namespace - `"host"`: use the host system's IPC namespace If not specified, daemon default is used, which can either be `"private"` or `"shareable"`, depending on daemon version and configuration.
---@field Cgroup? string Cgroup to use for the container.
---@field Links? docker.HostConfigLinksList A list of links for the container in the form `container_name:alias`.
---@field OomScoreAdj? number An integer value containing the score given to the container in order to tune OOM killer preferences.
---@field PidMode? string Set the PID (Process) Namespace mode for the container. It can be either: - `"container:<name|id>"`: joins another container's PID namespace - `"host"`: use the host's PID namespace inside the container
---@field Privileged? boolean Gives the container full access to the host.
---@field PublishAllPorts? boolean Allocates an ephemeral host port for all of a container's exposed ports. Ports are de-allocated when the container stops and allocated when the container starts. The allocated port might be changed when restarting the container. The port is selected from the ephemeral port range that depends on the kernel. For example, on Linux the range is defined by `/proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_local_port_range`.
---@field ReadonlyRootfs? boolean Mount the container's root filesystem as read only.
---@field SecurityOpt? docker.HostConfigSecurityOptList A list of string values to customize labels for MLS systems, such as SELinux.
---@field StorageOpt? docker.HostConfigStorageOptMap Storage driver options for this container, in the form `{"size": "120G"}`.
---@field Tmpfs? docker.HostConfigTmpfsMap A map of container directories which should be replaced by tmpfs mounts, and their corresponding mount options. For example: ``` { "/run": "rw,noexec,nosuid,size=65536k" } ```
---@field UTSMode? string UTS namespace to use for the container.
---@field UsernsMode? string Sets the usernamespace mode for the container when usernamespace remapping option is enabled.
---@field ShmSize? number Size of `/dev/shm` in bytes. If omitted, the system uses 64MB.
---@field Sysctls? docker.HostConfigSysctlsMap|distilled.Null A list of kernel parameters (sysctls) to set in the container. This field is omitted if not set.
---@field Runtime? string|distilled.Null Runtime to use with this container.
---@field Umask? number|distilled.Null Set the initial umask for a Unix container. If omitted, the daemon does not set a umask in the OCI process specification, and the runtime's default behavior applies. JSON integers are decimal. For example, use `18` for the octal umask `0022`, and `511` for the octal umask `0777`.
---@field Isolation? docker.HostConfigIsolation|string Isolation technology of the container. (Windows only)
---@field MaskedPaths? docker.HostConfigMaskedPathsList The list of paths to be masked inside the container (this overrides the default set of paths).
---@field ReadonlyPaths? docker.HostConfigReadonlyPathsList The list of paths to be set as read-only inside the container (this overrides the default set of paths).

